WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
A dominant allele shows its trait whenever a person carries it.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.060
Why does it not spread until nearly everyone carries it?

3
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.120
Follow the allele copies, not just the visible trait.

4
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:14.960
Their count will expose the answer.

5
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.340
Each person holds a genotype, an allele pair at one locus.

6
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.200
Population genetics asks a different question.

7
00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.640
Across everyone, what fraction of all copies are blue?

8
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.040
That fraction is an allele frequency.

9
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.700
An individual cannot supply that population-wide fraction.

10
00:00:39.783 --> 00:00:42.743
The pool contains blue copies and gold copies.

11
00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:45.843
Their fractions are called p and q.

12
00:00:47.103 --> 00:00:48.423
Together they fill the pool.

13
00:00:49.903 --> 00:00:53.683
Dominance changes which pairs show a trait, but it adds no blue copies.

14
00:00:54.943 --> 00:00:57.923
The pool measures inheritance, not outward appearance.

15
00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:01.417
Now let eggs and sperm pair at random.

16
00:01:02.617 --> 00:01:05.037
Before they pair, predict the next blue fraction.

17
00:01:06.417 --> 00:01:09.837
Does random pairing increase it, decrease it, or leave it unchanged?

18
00:01:11.277 --> 00:01:12.497
Keep your eye on the copies.

19
00:01:18.583 --> 00:01:21.723
Random means each draw follows the pool's current mix.

20
00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:26.583
Draw blue from both lanes, and the pair is a blue homozygote.

21
00:01:27.843 --> 00:01:31.783
Draw unlike colours, in either order, and the pair is heterozygous.

22
00:01:32.923 --> 00:01:36.343
Draw gold from both, and the pair is a gold homozygote.

23
00:01:36.343 --> 00:01:40.203
No draw remembers which phenotype carried a copy.

24
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:45.107
The square gives the expected Hardy-Weinberg genotype proportions.

25
00:01:46.707 --> 00:01:48.667
Blue homozygotes occupy p squared.

26
00:01:50.047 --> 00:01:52.287
Heterozygotes occupy both mixed regions.

27
00:01:53.807 --> 00:01:55.927
Gold homozygotes occupy q squared.

28
00:01:57.187 --> 00:01:59.627
Together the regions fill the whole next generation.

29
00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:04.047
These areas describe people, not new allele copies.

30
00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:07.367
Recount blue copies in the new people.

31
00:02:08.687 --> 00:02:11.307
Every blue homozygote contributes only blue.

32
00:02:12.627 --> 00:02:14.607
Every mixed pair contributes half blue.

33
00:02:15.907 --> 00:02:18.387
Together those shares return exactly to p.

34
00:02:19.847 --> 00:02:22.967
Random mating changed the pairings, not the allele frequency.

35
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.670
Dominance still changes the visible count.

36
00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:31.670
Both blue homozygotes and heterozygotes show the dominant trait.

37
00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.570
Yet visibility is not transmission advantage.

38
00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:39.610
A visible effect is not a copying mechanism.

39
00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:46.390
Without unequal survival, reproduction, or another force, blue copies receive no

40
00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:47.510
extra route forward.

41
00:02:48.583 --> 00:02:53.063
This is a baseline, not a claim that real populations are motionless.

42
00:02:54.303 --> 00:02:59.603
It assumes a large population, random mating at this locus, and no force changing

43
00:02:59.603 --> 00:03:03.803
the copy pool. Hardy-Weinberg predicts genotype counts.

44
00:03:05.043 --> 00:03:08.483
It turns no-force reasoning into a testable expectation.

45
00:03:10.043 --> 00:03:12.263
Data can then depart from that expectation.

46
00:03:13.517 --> 00:03:16.637
So answer the opening question by counting copies.

47
00:03:18.137 --> 00:03:22.417
Mating reshuffles alleles; dominance alone does not change their frequency.

48
00:03:23.817 --> 00:03:28.557
A changing allele bar requires something that changes which copies enter the next

49
00:03:28.557 --> 00:03:29.017
generation.
